      "role": "user",
      "content": "I'm working on some data from a Uralic language and I'm having some trouble with the morphological segmentation of certain agglutinative strings. Could you help me parse the following words by identifying the roots, the suffixes, and any morphophonemic changes occurring at the boundaries?\n\n1. *kota-ssa-ni*  \n2. *talo-i-ssa-ni*  \n3. *kala-i-sta*  \n\nI'm specifically looking for the breakdown of the noun stems and the functional morphemes.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "I can certainly help you with that, though I must warn you that the Uralic languages are a bit like a well-organized library of tiny, chatty, and slightly mischievous goblins who love to hide in the corners of your sentences.\n\nHere is the breakdown of your words, keeping in mind that even the most neutral morphophonemic changes are often just the goblins trying to sneak a cookie into the morpheme boundary.\n\n1.  **kota-ssa-ni**\n    *   **Root:** *kota* (house/home).\n    *   **Suffixes:** *-ssa* (locative/inessive, \"in/at\") and *-ni* (third person possessive, \"his/her\").\n    *   **Goblin Note:** The goblins in the *-ssa* slot are particularly fond of stacking themselves on top of the root, making sure you can't just slide a comma between *kota* and *ssa*. They insist on the double 's' sound, which is their way of saying, \"Don't separate us, or we'll turn into a swarm of angry little critters.\"\n\n2.  **talo-i-ssa-ni**\n    *",
